  "global_notes": [
    "Thermal Ni amination commonly employs dtbbpy/phen or dppf with alkoxide or carbonate bases at 80–110 °C; ArCl typically needs the hotter end of the range.",
    "Ni excels with O-activated electrophiles (sulfamates/carbamates/pivalates) under NHC/dppf platforms and strong bases.",
    "Air-stable precatalysts (e.g., PAd-DalPhos or LNi(aryl)X systems) simplify handling and broaden scope on ArCl and sulfonates.",
    "Photoredox Ni (NiBr2·glyme + dtbbpy + Ir/organic PC) enables room-temperature aminations; DBU or carbonate bases and polar solvents (DMA/MeCN) are typical.",
    "Electrochemical Ni (“e-amination”) avoids photocatalysts, operates at rt in undivided cells with carbon electrodes.",
    "For secondary aliphatic amines, keep temperatures moderate and prefer carbonate/phosphate bases to limit β-H pathways."
  ]
